# Shell scripts must keep LF endings regardless of platform.
#
# This repo is developed on Windows with core.autocrlf=true, which rewrites
# checked-out files to CRLF. A shell script that comes back with CRLF has
# "#!/bin/sh\r" as its shebang, which is not a valid interpreter path on Unix,
# so the script fails to execute. For .githooks/ that would silently disable
# the embargo guard on a fresh clone, which is the exact failure mode the
# tracked hook exists to prevent.
.githooks/* text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf

# Everything under site/ is served as bytes by Workers Static Assets, so the
# checked-out line endings ARE the published line endings. With
# core.autocrlf=true a plain checkout rewrote site/llms.txt to CRLF, the next
# deploy shipped it, and every section name the reference llms.txt parser
# returned carried a trailing "\r" again, three days after it had been fixed
# by hand. A by-hand fix cannot survive a branch switch; this can.
site/** text eol=lf
# Images are bytes. The site/** text rule above must never touch them: the
# first attempt at that rule staged og.png, favicon.ico and
# apple-touch-icon.png as "modified", which is what a corrupting rewrite looks
# like one step before it happens.
site/**/*.png binary
site/**/*.ico binary
site/**/*.gif binary
site/**/*.jpg binary
